 * **Retry rule of the senders in this repository.** The burst script, the portal demo's burst and the load driver (`load_tools/load_driver.py`) retry a refused connection or a 503 by TIME, not by count: the first wait is 0.1 s, each wait doubles up to 1 s, the desk's `Retry-After` is honoured up to that 1 s, and no new try starts more than 30 s after the first (`BURST_BACKOFF_SECONDS`, `BURST_BACKOFF_MAX_SECONDS`, `BURST_RETRY_DEADLINE_SECONDS` in `constants.py`; tests in `tests/test_burst_retry.py`). `max_retries=0` in the burst script means no retry at all.
-* Of the connections the desk did receive in the larger bursts, some were answered 503 `busy` (database lock wait over). A 503 from a lock wait may have left an open receipt; a 503 from the connection limit does not (it is written at accept time in `src/lead_desk/server.py`, before the request is read, without calling the desk). In both cases send the same request number again. In the author's own larger bursts every connection the desk received was answered with JSON (no log is shipped), and `tests/test_http.py::test_ten_parallel` checks that only one email is sent for the lead (the burst command itself only counts the answers it got and prints how many were 201 and 200; it does not read the mail count).
+* Of the connections the desk did receive in the larger bursts, some were answered 503 `busy` (database lock wait over). A 503 from a lock wait may have left an open receipt; a 503 from the connection limit does not (it is written at accept time in `src/lead_desk/server.py`, before the request is read, without calling the desk). In both cases send the same request number again. In the author's own larger bursts every connection the desk received was answered with JSON (no log is shipped), and `tests/test_http.py::test_ten_parallel` checks that only one email is sent for the lead (the burst command itself only counts the answers it got: it prints how many answers of each status came back and whether the number of 200, 201 and 202 answers equals the number expected; it does not read the mail count).
 * The demo HubSpot stand-in (`demo_only/fake_hubspot.py`, made-up data) reads a request body before it answers, so a late body is not reset on Windows. The exception is a Content-Length that is negative, not a plain number, longer than 7 digits or above 1 MiB: it answers 400 at once WITHOUT reading the body (reading would mean reading until the client stops); a client that really sends 1 MiB or more may therefore see a connection reset instead of the 400. A 5000-digit Content-Length is such a 400 (a digit string that long cannot be turned into a number by Python). Tests: `tests/test_codeant_fakes.py`.
 * A request body shorter than the Content-Length it announced: the desk answers 408 `request_timeout` when the body has not fully arrived 10 seconds after the headers ended (`BODY_READ_DEADLINE_SECONDS`) if the sender keeps the connection open, or 400 if the sender closes it; the 408 and the 400 are both counted in `rejected_envelopes` when the stall or close happens in the body of `POST /leads`; a stall while the headers are still arriving gets 408 but is not counted. A Content-Length over the size cap, however long the number, is answered 413 straight away; this holds for the decision body of `POST /review/<id>/resolve` too (after the Host and Origin check). Two different Content-Length values in one request are answered 400. A request target that cannot be parsed is answered 400.
 * **Known gaps in these figures.** The larger bursts depend on the machine and were not repeated for every version of the desk or with every sender retry rule; treat them as indications for the machine they ran on.
